19
00:02:44,800 --> 00:03:05,668
And what happens in the production is that we build up the chip in layers, one layer at a time, and we use the um what we call the lithography machines to etch a pattern on the wafer surface.

20
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:24,260
and then we use other tools that are made by other people to deposit material on these wafers, to etch material off, to change the surface, to add small amounts of impurities to the surface, and so on.

21
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:32,068
And this requires hundreds, if not thousands, of individual steps for every single chip.

22
00:03:33,504 --> 00:03:37,424
Now, our role in this is that we make these lithography machines.

23
00:03:38,064 --> 00:03:41,444
When you see them by themselves, everything looks simple.

24
00:03:41,664 --> 00:03:46,864
There's this big white featureless box.

25
00:03:47,664 --> 00:03:55,744
Wafers are fed in, already coated with a chemical that is reactive to light.

26
00:03:56,304 --> 00:04:03,812
Something happens inside the machine, and exposed wafers come out ready for the next step of processing.

27
00:04:05,134 --> 00:04:18,224
Now, inside that machine, what happens is that the wafers are loaded in, they're initially measured, so we're looking at just tiny distortions in the surface of the wafer.

28
00:04:18,464 --> 00:04:23,984
So they might be slightly stretched, they might be slightly compressed, or so on in the machine.

29
00:04:24,464 --> 00:04:27,264
