80
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:39,504
So going back to us and looking at what we changed and why, it's a little bit of a story, but it starts off with our idea of a measurement sequence.

81
00:10:39,664 --> 00:10:41,504
And initially this was really, really simple.

82
00:10:41,584 --> 00:10:51,952
It was okay, just measure the height of the wafer, measure the horizontal positions, measure some fiducials, done next wafer light it up.

83
00:10:51,952 --> 00:10:58,384
And for a while, you know, our first systems had a very simple way of executing the measurements.

84
00:10:58,734 --> 00:11:02,064
But over time, we started to get more and more complicated.

85
00:11:02,384 --> 00:11:07,652
So our drivers for this were really speed and concatenation of actions.

86
00:11:07,886 --> 00:11:16,292
So we started doing things in parallel, we started trying to squeeze all of our communications to fit into the shadow of the hardware moves.

87
00:11:16,864 --> 00:11:30,820
Um we we started really tweaking things to just try and get the last bit of time out of our system in order to get basically, the more wafers it prints, the more money the customer makes.

88
00:11:32,462 --> 00:11:35,824
Then we also started looking more at our robustness.

89
00:11:36,144 --> 00:11:41,184
So, as you can imagine, measurements can fail, things can go wrong, things can be out of spec.

90
00:11:41,984 --> 00:11:44,704
And this really sort of falls into three buckets.

91
00:11:45,264 --> 00:11:57,824
firstly, there's just trying to successfully measure the um the current wafer as quickly as possible to keep production running as fast as we can.

92
00:11:58,544 --> 00:12:08,356
And if that fails, then we still try and see, okay, is there a way of getting enough measurement that we can still continue with this wafer?

93
00:12:09,038 --> 00:12:13,764
You know, because this wafer it's an expensive piece of material, we don't want to throw it away.

94
00:12:14,318 --> 00:12:17,104
And then the final part of it is, okay.

95
00:12:17,984 --> 00:12:35,588
if we really cannot expose the current wafer, you know, if there's something critically wrong with it, then, uh, you know, can we quickly clear it out of our system, get the next wafer into the system, and carry on production?

96
00:12:37,998 --> 00:12:45,956
And then finally, our third drive of this was you know we started adding new features, new ways of doing modeling.

97
00:12:46,350 --> 00:12:51,268
Some of these new ways are commercial options, some of them are customer switchable.

98
00:12:52,896 --> 00:13:02,496
some of them require more calculation time, which feeds back to the original concerns about speed in the system.

99
00:13:03,054 --> 00:13:08,292
Some of them may have um you know um differences in terms of their robustness.

100
00:13:08,656 --> 00:13:22,896
So basically, all of these things wound up making what started as a really, really simple shopping list into something that became this sort of monstrous monolithic function with a huge fan out.

101
00:13:24,014 --> 00:13:27,856
You don't need to see the details of this, but you, you can just see there that.

102
00:13:27,936 --> 00:13:28,176
okay.

103
00:13:28,256 --> 00:13:30,416
this thing calls lots and lots and lots of things.

104
00:13:30,574 --> 00:13:33,376
and there are lots and lots and lots of branches inside it.

105
00:13:33,616 --> 00:13:37,376
and the cyclomatic complexity of that then explodes.

106
00:13:37,456 --> 00:13:45,296
and it explodes to the point where the cyclomatic complexity is almost meaningless in this universe.

107
00:13:45,776 --> 00:13:49,696
you know, you wind up with so many branches, you say so many paths through here.

108
00:13:50,766 --> 00:13:56,416
it's not meaningful to the number of scanners and the number of wafers we produce.

109
00:13:56,656 --> 00:13:58,980
It's not even meaningful on this scale.

110
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:05,536
So, and this is still sort of maybe 15, 20 years ago.

111
00:14:05,696 --> 00:14:07,376
Um, how are we dealing with this?

112
00:14:07,536 --> 00:14:22,896
Well, we then said, well, okay, we've got to a point where complexity is so high that we can't really secure the regression of making yet more changes and adding more complexity to the system.

113
00:14:23,456 --> 00:14:34,816
And so, as an example say, well, we started with the NXT1 measure sequence, so this was just a particular machine type, it doesn't really matter which one.

114
00:14:35,790 --> 00:14:52,976
and then when we introduced the next major variant, we added some new features, we made the wafer Z map significantly faster, we added some fallback mechanisms, basically we created a new sequence.

115
00:14:53,376 --> 00:14:56,736
And so maybe you can tell where this is going.

116
00:14:59,470 --> 00:15:00,576
and that sort of worked.

117
00:15:00,656 --> 00:15:04,096
It meant that we didn't have new regression on the systems in the field.

118
00:15:05,296 --> 00:15:23,296
Then we introduced NXT3, which added completely new sensor types, and we wanted an even faster sequence, so we built the NXT3 sequence, which is our fastest ever sequence, the one that's sort of running at over 300 wafers an hour these days.

119
00:15:23,616 --> 00:15:34,416
And we also thought, okay, but we want to really try and improve our most robust saved the wafer at any cost sequence, so we extended that.

120
00:15:34,976 --> 00:15:49,216
And yeah, you you you you get the idea that basically we had replaced one complexity rabbit hole, gotten out of it, and then fallen straight into the next one.

121
00:15:49,536 --> 00:15:58,336
And then EUV came along, and the EUV we said, oh well, this system runs in vacuum, which means that it's got completely different thermal characteristics.

122
00:15:58,496 --> 00:16:04,576
We want to change the order of executing all our measurements around the uh the thermal expansion of the system.

123
00:16:05,056 --> 00:16:06,896
And stop.

124
00:16:08,910 --> 00:16:14,628
There was a point where we just said, well, wait a minute, this is just too much to continue doing this.

125
00:16:15,910 --> 00:16:23,204
And it was at that point we made the decision to do something that was kind of expensive and scary, but to start over.

126
00:16:24,270 --> 00:16:31,296
So at that point we um we we really thought, okay, let's think about a new decomposition of our software.

127
00:16:31,616 --> 00:16:50,496
And some of my colleagues, like uh Sven Weber or uh uh Eva Horst have given external presentations on the sort of guts of this, but basically what we did was we separated our software into three types of components.

128
00:16:50,670 --> 00:17:01,296
So it's similar to what was at that point um uh being discussed in domain-driven or model-driven engineering.

129
00:17:01,696 --> 00:17:04,176
And start at the top with the control.

130
00:17:04,910 --> 00:17:12,016
Previously, we had this sort of monolithic sort of control structure that really had a sort of list like flow through it.

131
00:17:13,006 --> 00:17:31,232
We've replaced that with an event -based architecture using finite state machines, and there, um, you know, the, um, the core of this control code was that it is always responsive.

132
00:17:31,406 --> 00:17:35,392
It didn't have sort of internal guts of durative actions;

133
00:17:35,632 --> 00:17:40,964
it didn't have, uh, you know, any awareness of the data going through the system.

134
00:17:41,518 --> 00:18:15,716
And so there we, uh, initially we started with ASD, later, we moved towards Coco, and we find that doing that meant that we could do formal verification of a lot of this control logic, which really meant that a lot of things, a lot of the bugs that we had in our older code were just not really possible anymore because the tooling would detect and throw an error before generating the C code that we'd compile as our binaries.

135
00:18:16,592 --> 00:18:19,312
One thing you cannot waver is the compiler.

136
00:18:21,422 --> 00:18:44,512
With respect to our algorithms there, we were pretty religious on keeping these stateless, very sort of self-contained atomic actions, which then meant that we could really test these, build them and so on, completely in isolation of everything else.

137
00:18:44,832 --> 00:18:49,152
So almost all of this is tested through unit level testing.

138
00:18:49,312 --> 00:18:54,660
We don't need to sort of higher level testing apart from sort of a few integration tests.

139
00:18:55,214 --> 00:19:12,352
And then finally, the data, we actually wound up building a tool ourselves to generate the C code from models of our data structures and the relationships between them, and then.

140
00:19:12,590 --> 00:19:19,552
you know, it contains the sort of usual things like the, uh, crowd operators, navigation, and so on.

