00:26:18,016 --> 00:26:27,776
and even now we're just starting that ramping up of high NA EUV, so the very latest systems.

186
00:26:27,936 --> 00:26:30,244
There are only a handful of these in the world at the moment.

187
00:26:31,216 --> 00:26:39,972
And what we see is that, uh customers' cadence, here we have a bit of a problem.

188
00:26:41,230 --> 00:26:50,564
some of our customers are still working on entire processes that only use eyeline or maybe use krypton fluoride.

189
00:26:51,376 --> 00:27:02,756
but some of our customers who are making things like the AI chips or the latest mobile phone chips or so on, they're using a mixture of all of these systems.

190
00:27:03,310 --> 00:27:11,876
And they used a mixture because basically the tools at the bottom they cannot do the fine-grained patterning, but they are cheap.

191
00:27:13,550 --> 00:27:22,340
whereas the systems up at the top, the latest high-AEV system is over $300 million for a single tool.

192
00:27:23,296 --> 00:27:31,428
so you know, you can imagine someone with um, you could say, well, you could do everything with this really fancy new tool.

193
00:27:32,496 --> 00:27:44,176
but it would be like if a woodworker were to use a scalpel for everything, yes, it will work, but when it comes to cutting down a tree, it really isn't the right tool for the job.

194
00:27:44,256 --> 00:27:48,576
It would work, and you could sit there slightly scraping away at the tree with it.

195
00:27:50,350 --> 00:28:00,496
and so where the customer is using all of these systems, then they really want all of these systems to behave in the same way.

196
00:28:00,656 --> 00:28:06,976
They want the same software interfaces to it, they want the same sort of overall behaviors of the system.

197
00:28:09,136 --> 00:28:20,576
because otherwise the complexity of their factual automation system gets bigger, and they wind up with the headache about the complexity rather than us.

198
00:28:21,536 --> 00:28:23,136
Maybe that'd be a good idea.

199
00:28:24,558 --> 00:28:34,416
but on the other hand, you've got these guys who've been running these older processes, and they want everything to just keep running as it does run.

200
00:28:34,576 --> 00:28:43,936
They don't want to have new APIs, new reports, new recipe information, and dot dot.

201
00:28:44,974 --> 00:28:50,628
You know, and they're really sort of Pulling in a completely different direction.

202
00:28:51,584 --> 00:29:06,144
This leads to what I tend to refer to as a conservation of misery problem, in the sense that either we make those guys unhappy by not providing the new interfaces to our older systems.

203
00:29:06,304 --> 00:29:22,464
or we make these guys unhappy, in which case, we're saying, "Well, you have to change your working automation system, Or you make everyone happy by picking something in the middle, or we absorb the complexity ourselves, in which case we're unhappy.

204
00:29:22,734 --> 00:29:23,984
but at least the customers are happy.

205
00:29:24,064 --> 00:29:25,188
and the other guys paying.

206
00:29:27,504 --> 00:29:39,428
Now the next thing to sort of bear in mind, and yeah, I sort of go back to what I said about the fab, the factory, you know, this $20 billion machine.

207
00:29:40,704 --> 00:29:59,664
previously I showed, okay, how big a part is measure-side of the scanner, but if you look on the scale of things, um, you know, this latest EUV scanner is kind of like this tiny little bit of this overall system.

208
00:30:00,704 --> 00:30:06,724
And this is probably the sort of critical message that came across.

209
00:30:08,430 --> 00:30:16,932
at the end of the day, the customer is interested in the performance of the total system, not your part of the system.

210
00:30:18,064 --> 00:30:25,024
it doesn't matter if we make our scanner a little bit better, a little bit more precise, a little bit more accurate, and so on.

211
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:34,532
What matters is whether the wafers that come out of this sanctuary actually work and then can be sold to their customers.

212
00:30:36,064 --> 00:30:50,944
And so um, you you know, there's very much a slight difference of view there because you just say, oh well, yes, our system, we make it better, we make it faster, we'll make it a little bit more, and more, more.

213
00:30:51,584 --> 00:31:00,132
Um but if we change our behavior, then they potentially have to adapt around this.

214
00:31:01,104 --> 00:31:07,584
And you know, we live in a very hardware-centric world, but the same is true in other situations.

215
00:31:08,206 --> 00:31:14,304
You know, I would take the example of when uh ChatGPT-5 was released.

216
00:31:14,384 --> 00:31:22,596
When they initially released it, they did it without warning, and just one morning it's like, oh, you were running with four something, now you've got five.

217
00:31:23,502 --> 00:31:25,988
And some people, and it's better, it's better, it's better.

218
00:31:27,534 --> 00:31:31,664
Some people said, well, wait a minute, it's better, but it's different.

219
00:31:31,744 --> 00:31:37,184
And so some of my workflows either don't work or don't flow.

220
00:31:38,140 --> 00:31:39,364
And now I have a problem.

221
00:31:40,464 --> 00:31:49,252
and similarly for us, if we correct for something in our machine that we didn't correct for before, it could be that our customer was already correcting for it somewhere else.

222
00:31:53,024 --> 00:32:00,388
and as I mentioned, any production time loss is money that they are not making.

223
00:32:00,878 --> 00:32:13,744
Yeah, this is they they have a huge amount of cash tied up in this this lab, um, they have a huge amount of running costs, huge numbers of people, huge electricity bills.

224
00:32:15,184 --> 00:32:22,820
and so if for some reason we disrupt that production because suddenly they say, oh.

225
00:32:25,870 --> 00:32:37,200
yes, you've made this better in some sense, but to us you've just made it different, then they start losing out.

226
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:47,680
so an analogy I'd use is to say, you know, if I offered you, okay, tomorrow morning I can give you 20% more horsepower in your car for free.

227
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:49,280
I will go and do it overnight.

228
00:32:49,360 --> 00:32:50,980
You don't have to worry about it.

229
00:32:51,520 --> 00:32:56,240
the next morning you get in your car, you try starting the engine, clunk, nothing happens instead.

230
00:32:56,400 --> 00:33:01,220
Uh because, yes, I've upgraded your engine, but it doesn't work with your gearbox anymore.

231
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:03,876
you'd be kind of annoyed at me.

232
00:33:04,080 --> 00:33:08,800
Um, you know, what does your car cost, you know, 30,000, 40,000 euros?

233
00:33:09,102 --> 00:33:16,932
If it's 100 million euros, then you are going to be a little bit past being just angry.

234
00:33:18,510 --> 00:33:36,400
Now, the sort of things that can go wrong with this, and this is something that's maybe specific to machines that are as long-lived as ours, So there are machines that are in the field that have been running since before I started my career.

235
00:33:38,606 --> 00:33:44,484
everything that the customer can observe can become a requirement.

236
00:33:44,974 --> 00:33:53,188
Not one that you captured, not one that you wrote down, not one that you promised, but the customer, their aim is to get everything they can out of the machine.

237
00:33:53,902 --> 00:34:02,080
An analogy I use there is it's like we build Formula One cars of the lithography business and TSMC and Samsung and Intel and so on.

238
00:34:02,160 --> 00:34:05,920
They're the Leon Verstappen's and Lewis Hamilton's of the world.

239
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:08,560
They really know how to drive these machines.

240
00:34:08,782 --> 00:34:11,680
They know more about how these systems drive than we do.

241
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:18,080
And so they're constantly looking to try and scrape a little bit more out of it.

242
00:34:18,254 --> 00:34:18,880
Just a little bit.

243
00:34:19,200 --> 00:34:23,396
One more chip perway is worth millions of Euros.

244
00:34:23,840 --> 00:34:42,160
And so, they will sort of monitor the system, they will data mine all of the information that comes out of the system, and correlating that to yield, they will do experiments, they will do anything with any information that they can get their hands on.

245
00:34:42,720 --> 00:34:44,080
this one really burned us.

246
00:34:44,160 --> 00:34:56,560
It included things like diagnostic logs that we built for our own use, for looking at how the system is operating and really diagnosing the system.

247
00:34:56,720 --> 00:35:02,400
And then the customer started finding, ah, but there's stuff in here that correlates to yield, so we will start using this information.

248
00:35:02,766 --> 00:35:18,400
Later, we change our software architecture, and we say, Oh, well, we want to change some of that diagnostic information, because it's like intermediate results, things that now are calculated differently, and the customer says no.

249
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:34,372
and if you want to, whether it's block it, encrypt it, change it, whatever, they're the guys who say, no, I already have a fab that is working, I'm using this data.

250
00:35:35,758 --> 00:35:42,404
My old systems are producing this data, your new system, yes, it costs 100 million euros.

251
00:35:43,086 --> 00:35:46,640
I'm not paying for it unless I have this information.

252
00:35:46,800 --> 00:35:48,100
I need this to run.

253
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:52,196
I need this particular view, I need this dial on my dashboard.

254
00:35:53,710 --> 00:36:06,884
and so that actually was probably one of the individual most painful realizations was that okay, we really had to work a lot with our customers to to get around this.

255
00:36:09,006 --> 00:36:10,628
also it can include your bugs.

256
00:36:11,600 --> 00:36:13,540
and this is kind of a surprising one.

257
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:21,280
so if you have some behavior and the customer is observing this behavior, and they're saying this is how the system runs.

258
00:36:21,840 --> 00:36:23,280
I have an example.

259
00:36:23,440 --> 00:36:26,852
The DDOs are not so important, but it gives a flavor of it.

260
00:36:28,974 --> 00:36:34,960
looking at one of our wafer maps, there was a particular combination of options.

261
00:36:35,374 --> 00:36:50,340
We didn't think it was that important, but it meant that some of the areas around the edge of the wafer would be exposed using extrapolated data rather than measured data.

262
00:36:51,648 --> 00:36:56,420
And at the point when this bug was introduced, it was a bug.

263
00:36:58,862 --> 00:37:10,116
It wasn't seen as particularly high priority because the difference between that extrapolation and the measured data was absolutely tiny.

264
00:37:10,608 --> 00:37:15,008
So, you know, we we had some more urgent things to deal with.

265
00:37:15,088 --> 00:37:17,028
So, okay, fine, we'll deal with it later.

266
00:37:17,838 --> 00:37:19,728
When we refactored our code, we dealt with it.

267
00:37:19,808 --> 00:37:23,568
We said, ah, all these little bugs, we're not going to replicate any of those.

268
00:37:23,888 --> 00:37:25,636
We're going to fix all of this.

269
00:37:27,726 --> 00:37:35,328
And then we found that some of our customers were actually using the fact that we weren't measuring those bits of the wafer.

270
00:37:35,662 --> 00:37:39,876
And they were using those areas to do some unusual processing.

271
00:37:40,334 --> 00:37:43,488
They never tell us exactly what it is, but it's something.

272
00:37:43,648 --> 00:37:54,276
And then basically they come back and they say, well, um, you know, yes, we're relying on you not measuring this part of the wafer.

273
00:37:54,928 --> 00:37:58,596
now our Fab defense system starts throwing up false positives.

274
00:37:59,808 --> 00:38:05,796
we need you to actually return back to that buggy behavior.

275
00:38:06,928 --> 00:38:11,408
And we went around with this customer for quite a few cycles.

276
00:38:11,488 --> 00:38:23,972
and eventually we actually put a switch into our system to enable the buggy behavior, because otherwise they would have to re-qualify their entire process.

277
00:38:24,494 --> 00:38:28,288
and that included not just re-qualifying it with respect to our machines.

278
00:38:28,494 --> 00:38:29,808
but all the other machines in the fab.

279
00:38:29,968 --> 00:38:31,328
So this would be a huge cost to them.

280
00:38:31,408 --> 00:38:37,988
And they just say, oh no, no, no, no, we're not accepting your new software while it doesn't have this bug in it.

281
00:38:41,488 --> 00:38:44,048
really, really, really upset me at the time.

282
00:38:44,208 --> 00:38:45,648
These days I understand it.

283
00:38:46,528 --> 00:38:48,528
Okay, okay, okay.

284
00:38:49,008 --> 00:38:59,248
Um so the sorts of things we started doing in terms of okay, how do we get our customers to accept this?

285
00:38:59,790 --> 00:39:17,316
The most important one, and you know, I gave the example of the AI model, rather, as an example of someone else who's got this wrong, is giving the customer a lot more advanced warning about any changes that we make.

286
00:39:18,894 --> 00:39:28,004
Although we like to try and develop our software in a more agile fashion, we still have to sort of stop at a certain point and start telling them, "Okay.

287
00:39:28,288 --> 00:39:32,448
this is how the new system is going to work .". And we have to make that promise.

288
00:39:32,528 --> 00:39:53,668
and it has to be, something of the order of six months, maybe even a year in advance, to give them time to actually start making changes that they will need to make to their automation system, so that when you finally deliver, they can actually use the system in its new state.

289
00:39:57,248 --> 00:40:08,368
we found that we also had to really tie these improvements to tangible improvements in the system behavior.

290
00:40:08,928 --> 00:40:10,288
I need to speed up.

291
00:40:12,688 --> 00:40:22,404
and it's not just about the money, it's also about the fact that at their end they have to request resources to improve the automation system behind it.

292
00:40:23,088 --> 00:40:28,900
So they have to make a sort of internal sales pitch to get the changes done.

293
00:40:31,910 --> 00:40:33,936
We started using a lot more simulation.

294
00:40:34,016 --> 00:40:52,416
We have this what we call digital twin scan now that enables us to run the actual production measurements through a virtual machine running new software or with new options on so that the customer can see before and after what the differences are.

295
00:40:52,576 --> 00:40:58,884
They can do that over an extended period, they can really evaluate without turning it on in production yet.

296
00:41:01,856 --> 00:41:14,976
And where it's not possible, where we really, really are stuck, then we go back to okay, we will use configurations and switches, we will take the extra pain on the complexity.

297
00:41:15,246 --> 00:41:17,380
There is a point where you just have to say, okay, hands up.

298
00:41:18,976 --> 00:41:21,796
So last slide, key takeaways.

299
00:41:22,016 --> 00:41:24,256
Good judgment comes from experience.

300
00:41:24,782 --> 00:41:27,696
Experience does come from some bad judgments.

301
00:41:27,936 --> 00:41:30,416
That is, yes, it is.

302
00:41:30,656 --> 00:41:38,372
Um but first key takeaway is do not stop your customer operation.

303
00:41:39,056 --> 00:41:41,456
that is the thing that all of this is about.

304
00:41:41,646 --> 00:41:44,164
That is the thing that's making somebody some money.

305
00:41:44,416 --> 00:41:51,936
Um and you know, that's the thing that when they pay hundreds of millions of euros, they demand it still works.

306
00:41:52,526 --> 00:41:58,496
the second most important message is do not stop the customer operation.

307
00:41:58,816 --> 00:42:01,616
I could not stress it enough, so I stressed it twice.

308
00:42:01,856 --> 00:42:13,856
Um, and that means really sort of, uh, things like giving them as much warning as they can so that they can adapt, using simulation, allowing them time to adapt.

309
00:42:13,936 --> 00:42:22,576
So even when you've introduced the new software, you make the old software available for quite an extended time, knowing that it will take them some time;

310
00:42:22,736 --> 00:42:24,708
maybe they will have bugs and problems.

311
00:42:27,086 --> 00:42:27,940
be careful.

312
00:42:28,910 --> 00:42:38,052
Everything that your system does can wind up becoming an unrecognized and unrecorded requirement.

313
00:42:38,816 --> 00:42:51,620
And if you only take away one thing from this, do not take for granted that making your system in isolation better makes the entire ecosystem better that it sits in.

314
00:42:52,462 --> 00:43:00,496
And with that, I would like to say thank you very much for your time this morning and thank you for the chat.

315
00:43:01,056 --> 00:43:01,856
Thank you.

